WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:19.560
Okay, welcome back. Welcome to video
number 20. And we're going to move on from
the idea generation process now. We're
going to move on to the second stage,
which is extremely crucial, called
gatekeeping.

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:45.620
Now, gatekeeping is a process that is
essentially used as a barrier between your
ideas and your real capital. It's you
putting up a barrier to try and stop
yourself doing silly things, as in, taking
an idea and just putting the idea on
straight away.

00:00:45.620 --> 00:01:07.660
So, let's run through this quickly again.
This concept that your ideas at this stage
are just ideas. They're not trades. A good
idea might end up being a bad trade. And a
bad idea will usually just end up becoming
a bad trade.

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:23.500
So, when we go, when we have a look at
this, let's, let's think of the example
where we think we've got a really, really
good idea. And we buy a stock or an asset
at 100.

00:01:24.500 --> 00:01:41.680
And we decide that if it trades at 90,
we're just going to trade out. So, it goes
from 100 to 90 and we trade out. We just
put the idea on straight away. And it just
goes to 90 almost immediately, within
days.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:49.140
So, we end up being forced to trade out.
So, we end up being forced to trade out.
We essentially get stopped out. The trade
hits our stock loss.

00:01:50.740 --> 00:02:02.200
But then, that asset goes from 90 to 400.
What you've actually just done there as a
trader is the worst thing you can possibly
do, which is you had a good idea.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:08.780
You put it on straight away. You put it on
straight away. You mistimed it. And you
were proved to be right, but you actually
lost money.

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:18.840
That's why we have the barrier of
gatekeeping to stop us mistiming good
trades.

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:32.760
But it's also to stop us entering bad
trades. So, imagine you buy this asset at
100. It trades at 90. And you trade out.
And then, this asset just goes to zero.

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:40.200
Well, that's all fine that you overlaid
some risk management onto it by stopping
yourself out at 90.

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:50.180
But you could have actually saved yourself
10 by just having a gatekeeping process in
place, so you didn't actually enter the
trade at all.

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:57.880
We're looking here at somebody generating
an idea and just putting the trade on
straight away.

00:02:58.280 --> 00:03:05.180
This is why we have a gatekeeping process.
And the gatekeeping process is essentially
two things.

00:03:05.640 --> 00:03:13.980
Technical analysis. So, using technical
analysis to better time your trades and to
stop yourself entering bad trades.

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:22.340
And price action indicators. And the price
action indicator we're going to look at
here the most is the watch list.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:34.840
And the processes you use in your watch
list to make sure that you enter good
trades in well-timed scenarios and you try
not to enter bad trades.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:40.300
So, whatever ends up in your portfolio
stands an extremely high probability of
making money.

00:03:41.700 --> 00:03:49.500
Now, up to this stage, what we've done is
we've generated an idea or multiple ideas
through the world view.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:56.300
And the world view has given us a view on
the stock market, commodities, currencies
and rates.

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:59.180
And within the stock market, we've drilled
down.

00:03:59.420 --> 00:04:09.760
We've gone from the index level through
the sector level to the stock level and
we've drilled from the top down and
combined that with some bottom-up analysis
on PE.

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:16.220
Now, we're moving on to gatekeeping, which
essentially is our idea cleansing phase.

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:20.300
Is the idea correct in terms of timing?

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:21.740
You might be early.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:22.900
You might be late.

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:24.500
It might be original.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:25.380
It might be unoriginal.

00:04:27.000 --> 00:04:35.460
The point here is to stop yourself putting
mistiming good trades and putting bad
trades on altogether.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:43.860
Now, a lot of retail traders consider
themselves useful and knowledgeable at
technical analysis.

00:04:44.860 --> 00:04:46.000
That's all fine.

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:51.160
You can put whatever technical analysis
you want to put into this process.

00:04:51.800 --> 00:04:56.960
But the main point here is that we're
looking at two things in terms of TA.

00:04:57.640 --> 00:05:00.740
Firstly, it comes in at the gatekeeping
stage.

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:03.260
It doesn't generate ideas for you.

00:05:03.580 --> 00:05:06.760
That's the first mistake that retail
traders always make.

00:05:07.320 --> 00:05:17.280
It's very, very easy to think that just
watching a line on a chart will give you
an idea when, in fact, the line is just
following the price anyway.

00:05:18.280 --> 00:05:20.920
The most important indicator is the price
itself.

00:05:21.920 --> 00:05:29.200
Secondly, it's the misinterpretation of
technical analysis or misinterpretation of
a chart when you pull it up.

00:05:30.480 --> 00:05:42.340
So, there's many, many scenarios that
we're going to go through in the
presentation where, when you look at
certain things in a chart, retail traders
often misinterpret what they're seeing.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:48.020
And that means it becomes unuseful rather
than useful as a tool.

00:05:48.820 --> 00:05:50.660
So, let's go to the presentation.

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:54.480
Let's take the first step in gatekeeping
and look at technical analysis.

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:59.360
And I'll see you at the end of video 20
with a summary of technical analysis TA.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:08.540
Okay, so let's move on to the stage of
gatekeeping.

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:15.880
So, let's assume now that you've got tons
and tons and tons of ideas.

00:06:17.180 --> 00:06:18.280
You've got a world view.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:21.060
You've got ideas in commodities,
currencies, rates.

00:06:22.500 --> 00:06:24.860
You've got ideas in sectors, stocks.

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:27.640
What do you do next?

00:06:30.060 --> 00:06:32.340
Obviously, everybody thinks their ideas
are brilliant.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:37.980
No one shortlists a bunch of ideas because
they're all rubbish.

00:06:39.300 --> 00:06:40.880
Everybody thinks their ideas are great.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:43.060
So, what do you do next?

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:49.560
You've got to put a barrier between
yourself and your capital.

00:06:49.820 --> 00:06:51.400
Your ideas and your capital.

00:06:53.800 --> 00:07:00.740
The idea of putting a barrier between your
ideas and your real money is to stop
yourself doing something stupid.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:07.520
What was the most annoying thing of being
a trader that we discussed yesterday?

00:07:10.680 --> 00:07:12.160
When you're right and you lose money.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:18.800
So, you buy a stock at 100 because you
think it's a great idea.

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:23.900
You get stopped out at 92 and then it goes
to 400.

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:35.780
If you have processes that put a barrier
between your ideas and your capital,
you're less likely to do stupid things.

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:44.080
So, what we're trying to do is go through
these processes to time things in the best
way possible.

00:07:46.300 --> 00:07:50.640
So, when things end up in our portfolio,
at least we've done the work.

00:07:50.740 --> 00:07:52.160
And if we're wrong, we're just wrong.

00:07:55.280 --> 00:07:58.700
It's better to be wrong and lose money
than lose money and be right.

00:08:01.080 --> 00:08:01.940
Does that make sense?

00:08:05.540 --> 00:08:06.320
So, what do we do?

00:08:06.380 --> 00:08:14.020
We apply a screening process of technical
analysis, TA, and price action indicators.

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:21.560
If all the planets are aligned and all
those boxes are ticked,

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:28.480
then we can seriously consider committing
capital to this idea, to an idea.

00:08:31.080 --> 00:08:33.620
So, this is what we're going to cover now.

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:36.260
Technical analysis in this part of the
process.

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:43.500
And TA is just one part of the gatekeeping
process to stop yourself doing something
stupid.

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:51.920
First, don't confuse technical analysis
with a tool that generates ideas.

00:08:52.940 --> 00:08:55.180
Retail traders are doing it in totally the
reverse.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:08:58.220
All the processes are now in reverse.

00:08:59.340 --> 00:09:00.740
So, what professional traders do.

00:09:01.520 --> 00:09:04.080
So, professional traders will start at the
top and work their way down,

00:09:04.520 --> 00:09:08.120
then pull up the chart to see if the chart
agrees with what they think.

00:09:10.480 --> 00:09:12.080
So, your fundamentals come first.

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:14.400
Your world view, your industry view, your
sector view.

00:09:15.900 --> 00:09:17.700
Your stock view, you pull up the chart.

00:09:19.620 --> 00:09:21.600
Not pull up the chart, take a position.

00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:24.000
When it goes wrong, then work out why.

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:27.480
Then justify it.

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:29.940
Then get a world view.

00:09:30.480 --> 00:09:31.520
And then put it in your pension.

00:09:31.780 --> 00:09:32.480
And it goes to zero.

00:09:35.900 --> 00:09:37.780
That's the reverse order of doing things.

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:43.380
So, don't confuse TA with a tool that
starts and generates you ideas.

00:09:44.540 --> 00:09:48.780
The vast majority of technical analysis
indicators react to the price.

00:09:50.600 --> 00:09:51.740
They follow the price.

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:54.960
The price itself is the indicator that's
important.

00:09:57.420 --> 00:09:59.040
So, it doesn't give you trade ideas.

00:09:59.360 --> 00:10:05.360
What it does is it gives you a pictorial
representation of what's happened in the
past.

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:13.440
I might give you some clues as to when you
can time your entry to the trade.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:16.920
It's not useless.

00:10:17.640 --> 00:10:18.540
It's quite useful.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:25.400
And we'll go through some of the TA stuff
that you can look at to time positions a
bit better.

00:10:29.140 --> 00:10:31.420
Try to think of it as a traffic light
system.

00:10:32.280 --> 00:10:35.060
Where you've got your fundamental view on
something.

00:10:35.660 --> 00:10:38.040
Where you think the world's going to go
up, for example.

00:10:38.960 --> 00:10:40.620
You think a sector's going to go up.

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:42.020
You think a stock's going to go up.

00:10:42.260 --> 00:10:43.320
Then you pull up the chart.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:49.660
And the chart literally says, there's no
way you should be buying this at the
moment.

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:56.420
It's a red in the traffic light system.

00:10:57.020 --> 00:10:58.460
Just stick it on your watch list.

00:10:59.160 --> 00:11:00.800
When the technicals move in your favour.

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:02.860
Then the timing is better.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:07.780
If you pull up the chart.

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:10.840
And technically everything agrees with
what you're thinking.

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:13.480
And it looks like a great entry point.

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:16.560
Don't be afraid to commit some capital to
it.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:20.640
It means it's a decent idea.

00:11:22.360 --> 00:11:23.220
If it's amber.

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:24.920
You could just be too early.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:26.140
Same as red.

00:11:27.020 --> 00:11:28.780
It can go on the watch list.

00:11:28.900 --> 00:11:30.380
Or you might take a starter position.

00:11:30.740 --> 00:11:32.280
What we call a half position.

00:11:32.400 --> 00:11:33.100
A starter position.

00:11:33.860 --> 00:11:36.540
Where you say, well my ideal size in this
situation.

00:11:37.000 --> 00:11:39.240
Is to maybe buy £10,000 worth.

00:11:40.520 --> 00:11:42.940
But at the moment the technicals are
neither here nor there.

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:46.140
So I'm going to buy £5,000.

00:11:47.140 --> 00:11:48.260
And just see what happens.

00:11:48.680 --> 00:11:50.460
And put quite a wide stop loss on the
trade.

00:11:50.940 --> 00:11:52.380
Give myself a chance of making money.

00:11:53.100 --> 00:11:55.900
The contribution of that position to the
rest of your portfolio.

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:56.920
Your portfolio should not hurt you.

00:11:58.660 --> 00:11:59.500
In the short term.

00:12:03.220 --> 00:12:04.480
Big mistakes of TA.

00:12:05.260 --> 00:12:06.140
First one we've covered.

00:12:07.080 --> 00:12:08.580
Getting a trade idea from a chart.

00:12:09.320 --> 00:12:10.260
It's totally amateur.

00:12:15.840 --> 00:12:23.960
Going short of an asset that you're
fundamentally bullish on because the
technicals are bearish.

00:12:26.120 --> 00:12:31.700
So you pull up the chart and the
technicals look like the stock is going
down.

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:36.460
But everything you've done up to this
point says that stock a year from now will
be higher.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:41.140
And you get convinced by the chart to
short it.

00:12:41.780 --> 00:12:42.960
And then it goes through the roof.

00:12:44.920 --> 00:12:45.680
Vice versa.

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:49.060
Going long an asset that you're
fundamentally bearish on.

00:12:49.700 --> 00:12:50.620
You pull up the chart.

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:56.520
It looks like technically everything's
great.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:58.860
And it looks like it's going to go up
forever.

00:12:59.940 --> 00:13:02.380
All the work to that point you've done is
bearish.

00:13:04.480 --> 00:13:06.600
And you get convinced by the chart to buy
some.

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:08.600
And then it halves.

00:13:11.800 --> 00:13:17.260
Again, no worse feeling in the world to
know the real situation, be right and use
money.

00:13:18.380 --> 00:13:19.880
This happens time and time again.

00:13:20.680 --> 00:13:21.880
Even in the professional world.

00:13:22.540 --> 00:13:24.140
That's the type of stuff you get fired
for.

00:13:25.800 --> 00:13:31.700
Where you walk into an office and ask for
an increase in capital because you've done
loads of work on the situation.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:34.040
And you're fundamentally bullish on it.

00:13:35.580 --> 00:13:38.220
And then the chart sucks you in to be
short.

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:43.700
And then you end up losing money.

00:13:44.520 --> 00:13:45.440
You'll get fired for it.

00:13:49.160 --> 00:13:54.400
So before you pull up your charts, you're
going to have fundamental predispositions.

00:13:55.560 --> 00:13:56.800
Now this is where you have to be careful.

00:13:58.980 --> 00:14:03.500
Because when you're fundamentally bullish
on something and the technicals aren't
quite there.

00:14:03.500 --> 00:14:06.500
Or fundamentally bearish on something and
the technicals aren't quite there.

00:14:08.020 --> 00:14:11.140
You're better not to participate at the
moment.

00:14:13.060 --> 00:14:18.760
But when you've got fundamental
predispositions, it's very difficult to
control those emotions to stop yourself
doing stupid things.

00:14:21.140 --> 00:14:23.120
You have to just stick it on the watch
list and wait.

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:24.260
And be patient.

00:14:25.680 --> 00:14:26.820
Nothing wrong with being patient.

00:14:27.320 --> 00:14:31.520
You can end up buying a stock higher than
where it was when you first started
looking at it.

00:14:31.940 --> 00:14:34.260
But the technicals are all in your favour.

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:37.860
The risk reward on that trade is now
better.

00:14:44.100 --> 00:14:47.980
So in terms of technicals, you can get as
complicated as you want.

00:14:48.880 --> 00:14:57.060
If you feel the need to get complicated on
technicals, and you know much more than me
about TA, which some of you probably will.

00:15:00.180 --> 00:15:01.960
Feel free to add it into this section.

00:15:05.540 --> 00:15:06.940
But see it for what it is.

00:15:09.260 --> 00:15:10.040
It's a chart.

00:15:12.280 --> 00:15:13.860
And the chart follows the price.

00:15:15.360 --> 00:15:17.280
All the technical indicators follow the
price.

00:15:17.860 --> 00:15:18.940
The price is the most important.

00:15:22.220 --> 00:15:24.740
So you can add any amount of technicals
into this point.

00:15:26.040 --> 00:15:26.860
Anywhere you want.

00:15:27.160 --> 00:15:27.680
That's fine.

00:15:29.160 --> 00:15:36.660
But make sure when you're looking at TA,
you treat it for what it is and put it in
the process at the right, where it
belongs.

00:15:37.720 --> 00:15:38.740
Don't work backwards.

00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:46.200
Start with technicals and then find out
fundamental reasons why you should be
doing something.

00:15:46.200 --> 00:15:47.840
Because the chart is telling you to do
something.

00:15:53.300 --> 00:15:58.800
So often technicals will tell you as much
as what not to do as to what to do.

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:06.620
So given your fundamental predisposition
of being bearish, do you short the stock
when you see this?

00:16:09.340 --> 00:16:10.600
No, you wait for it to break.

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:20.320
If your fundamental predisposition is
bullish, this confirms what you think.

00:16:21.340 --> 00:16:25.360
So you're now looking for entry around
trend, if you can get it.

00:16:29.160 --> 00:16:31.480
Trending stocks downwards.

00:16:31.780 --> 00:16:35.800
If your fundamental predisposition is
bullish, do you buy this?

00:16:36.660 --> 00:16:36.960
No.

00:16:37.480 --> 00:16:37.740
No.

00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:38.900
Stick it on the watch list.

00:16:39.280 --> 00:16:41.260
Wait for it to break up to the upside.

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:42.940
Wait for it to break the trend.

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:45.900
So what if it breaks in a month's time?

00:16:46.980 --> 00:16:51.620
Then the risk-reward on the trade is
probably better than it was before.

00:16:51.840 --> 00:16:55.760
But do you have to make a new fundamental
analysis of what you did a month ago?

00:16:56.100 --> 00:16:58.620
Yeah, you'll look at it again to see if
anything's changed.

00:17:01.400 --> 00:17:04.040
Is it worth buying at the bottom of the
channel?

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:06.640
Would that still be a valid entry?

00:17:07.840 --> 00:17:09.820
Well, who says it's the bottom of the
channel at that point?

00:17:11.300 --> 00:17:13.840
I mean, if I see a channel like this
and...

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:15.440
No, you're fighting the trend.

00:17:16.560 --> 00:17:18.860
You're fighting the trend because the
overall trend is down.

00:17:23.400 --> 00:17:28.620
If you've got a bullish fundamental
predisposition and this is the chart you
pull up,

00:17:29.660 --> 00:17:30.840
you're better off waiting.

00:17:31.080 --> 00:17:32.080
Just stick it on the watch list.

00:17:33.140 --> 00:17:37.040
What you're going to look at in the
context of all the opportunities in the
world, yeah?

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:41.160
40,000 stocks in the world, 40,000 assets
in the world, sorry,

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:43.680
equals 40,000 opportunities

00:17:43.680 --> 00:17:48.460
and actually 40,000 to the power of 39,999
opportunities

00:17:48.460 --> 00:17:50.060
because you have lots of spread rates.

00:17:50.520 --> 00:17:54.420
So there's literally a limitless amount of
opportunities in the world.

00:17:55.880 --> 00:17:58.060
If your fundamental predisposition on this
is bullish,

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:00.220
just time it better.

00:18:00.420 --> 00:18:04.120
Try and time it better by waiting for the
price to confirm what you're thinking.

00:18:06.520 --> 00:18:09.880
You see, we're trying to predict what's
going to happen in 6, 12 months' time.

00:18:10.360 --> 00:18:12.520
When you start seeing it slowly happening,

00:18:12.720 --> 00:18:17.280
that's when you become convinced that it's
actually getting to be a correct
situation, yeah?

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:23.560
What about ranging stocks or ranging
assets?

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:30.700
If you've got a fundamental predisposition
that's bullish,

00:18:30.920 --> 00:18:32.080
what do you do in this situation?

00:18:35.020 --> 00:18:35.380
Huh?

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:37.380
Half position.

00:18:37.380 --> 00:18:37.820
Half position?

00:18:39.820 --> 00:18:42.220
So would you say this is an amber on the
traffic lights?

00:18:46.240 --> 00:18:47.320
Who says it's an amber?

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:49.980
Who says it's a red?

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:53.740
It's a red because it hasn't broken from
the channel yet.

00:18:54.440 --> 00:18:55.460
Who says it's a green?

00:18:57.560 --> 00:18:58.700
You just go in full position.

00:19:01.160 --> 00:19:02.160
It's a bit subjective.

00:19:02.160 --> 00:19:03.440
It's a bit subjective, ranging stocks.

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:08.620
It's somewhere between watch list and a
half position.

00:19:13.620 --> 00:19:14.060
Why?

00:19:14.060 --> 00:19:14.080
Why?

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:25.760
Well, all that is telling me is that
someone in the market thinks that it's
cheap at 6.

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:26.840
That's why they're buying it.

00:19:29.040 --> 00:19:32.260
And someone in the market thinks it's
expensive at 8.

00:19:32.420 --> 00:19:33.460
That's why they're selling it.

00:19:34.460 --> 00:19:36.280
But there's no such thing as cheap and
expensive.

00:19:40.280 --> 00:19:46.400
So you might wait for it to break out
before you commit or you might commit a
little bit.

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:48.220
It doesn't really matter.

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:51.120
What that's telling you is that the
market's a bit screwed up at the moment.

00:19:55.720 --> 00:19:56.560
Does that make sense?

00:19:58.200 --> 00:19:59.240
Why is it screwed up?

00:19:59.320 --> 00:20:01.380
Because we know that there's no such thing
as cheap and expensive.

00:20:03.140 --> 00:20:05.180
That stock in 12 months' time could be at
$40.

00:20:06.460 --> 00:20:08.060
And that range is totally irrelevant.

00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:16.040
You'll see this time and time again in the
markets.

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:23.360
Head and shoulders formations where you've
got a left shoulder, a head, and a right
shoulder.

00:20:25.220 --> 00:20:26.740
What's actually happening in the price?

00:20:28.600 --> 00:20:31.860
Well, you've seen a bullish trend in the
past and you've had a blow off top.

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:35.600
And now the price momentum is slowing
down.

00:20:37.060 --> 00:20:40.760
And at this point here, it's broken what's
called the neckline.

00:20:44.060 --> 00:20:53.260
And in technical analysis, what you would
expect is a move to the downside that's
around the size of the head from the
neckline.

00:20:54.480 --> 00:21:00.600
So the head from the neckline, $8 to $11.

00:21:02.540 --> 00:21:04.100
So a drop of $3.

00:21:07.280 --> 00:21:13.200
If your fundamental predisposition is
bullish and you see this, and you're here,

00:21:14.060 --> 00:21:14.720
do you buy?

00:21:17.360 --> 00:21:17.760
No.

00:21:17.940 --> 00:21:18.800
Stick it on the watch list.

00:21:19.520 --> 00:21:23.960
If your fundamental predisposition is
bearish and it's broken the neckline,

00:21:25.040 --> 00:21:25.880
what do you do?

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:29.000
That's a bright green light.

00:21:30.120 --> 00:21:30.880
Go in big.

00:21:32.180 --> 00:21:34.020
And put your stop loss above the neckline.

00:21:37.060 --> 00:21:38.640
What about reverse head and shoulders?

00:21:42.060 --> 00:21:45.240
Your fundamental predisposition is
bearish.

00:21:46.680 --> 00:21:47.580
And you see this.

00:21:51.100 --> 00:21:52.240
Red, amber, or green?

00:21:53.820 --> 00:21:54.300
Red.

00:21:55.260 --> 00:21:56.160
Very bright red.

00:22:00.060 --> 00:22:01.100
It's going up $3.

00:22:04.220 --> 00:22:06.900
If your fundamental predisposition is
bullish, green.

00:22:09.100 --> 00:22:13.200
So again, it tells you as much what not to
do as what to do.

00:22:18.760 --> 00:22:21.740
When stocks put in double tops and double
bottoms.

00:22:24.600 --> 00:22:28.560
And then you see a break through a double
top and you see a break through previous
support.

00:22:32.560 --> 00:22:34.920
It's exactly the same way as everything
